{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1201,1440p' /Users/iv/.agents/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"make install            # all three binaries\nmake test               # run all tests\nmake snapshot           # goreleaser snapshot (cross-platform build)\nmake clean              # remove binaries + dist/\n```\n\n---\n---\n\n# Part 2: Planning\n\nPlan command reference, phase planning workflow, `.planning/` storage, visualization — see [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md).\n\nCanonical planning API:\n\n```bash\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan()'                          # whole project (all epics)\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'   # single node: epic/story planning model\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(TASK-01, mode=related)'     # single node: task/bug + dependency closure\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-01, STORY-02, TASK-03], mode=children)'  # set of nodes: union over their subtrees\n```\n\nScope forms:\n\n- `plan()` — whole project (all epics)\n- `plan(ID)` or `plan(scope=ID)` — single element scope (epic/story → children, task/bug → related closure by default)\n- `plan(scope=[ID, ID, ...])` — a set of epic/story/task/bug IDs; waves and critical path are computed over the union of the per-ID scopes. Unknown IDs are rejected together in one error. When per-ID default modes differ, the reported mode is `mixed`; pass `mode=children|related` to force one.\n\nCompact planning shape (`--format compact` + `plan(...)`) replaces the nested phase/element graph with an agent-friendly flat model — execution order without nested parsing:\n\n```\nactive:false\ncriticalPath:TASK-01 > TASK-02 > TASK-04\nelements:4\nmode:children\nscope:EPIC-01\nstatuses:{\"TASK-01\":\"done\",\"TASK-02\":\"development\",\"TASK-03\":\"backlog\",\"TASK-04\":\"backlog\"}\nwaves:[[\"TASK-01\",\"TASK-03\"],[\"TASK-02\"],[\"TASK-04\"]]\n```\n\n- `waves` — flat array of waves, each wave an ordered list of element IDs that can run in parallel\n- `criticalPath` — single-line ` > `-joined chain\n- `statuses` — minimal per-element fields (id → status only)\n\nThe default `--format json` plan output remains nested `scope`/`summary`/`phases`/`criticalPath`. Active out-of-scope blockers, dangling blocker IDs, and resolved elements with active blockers return a typed validation error that names the affected edge.\n\nLocal human-readable snapshot facade (presentation and persistence only):\n\n```bash\ntask-board plan                                    # full project plan\ntask-board plan EPIC-01                            # epic plan\ntask-board plan --save                             # save to file\n```\n\nAgents use `q plan()` above for phases/waves and the critical path; they never need to render a graph to plan. All render examples are centralized in [Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)](references/planning-workflow.md#human-visualization-explicit-request-only) and apply only when a human explicitly requests a graph.\n\n---\n\n# Part 3: Agent Tracking\n\n## Assigning Agents\n\n```bash\n# Assign agent to element\ntask-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1)'\n\n# Remove assignment\ntask-board m 'unassign(STORY-260218-abc)'\n```\n\n## Agent Dashboard\n\n```bash\n# Show active agents\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'\n\n# Custom stale threshold (minutes)\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents(stale=60)'\n```\n\nDashboard response includes each agent's name, assigned element list (`id`, `name`, `status`), and `totalAssigned`, plus top-level `totalAgents`. The `stale` parameter filters old `done` items; it does not return timestamps or a child-progress counter.\n\n## Sub-Agent Workflow\n\n1. Coordinator breaks work into stories, assigns agents:\n   ```bash\n   task-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1); assign(STORY-260218-def, agent=agent-2)'\n   ```\n2. Each sub-agent works on its scope, updating task statuses\n3. Coordinator monitors: `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\n4. When all done — agents disappear from dashboard\n\n## Agent Roles\n\nRoles define what an agent does, which skills it needs, what statuses it drives, and its definition of done.\n\n### Role Registry\n\nRoles are defined in `.roles/<role-name>/` directories:\n\n```\n.roles/\n├── developer/\n│   ├── role.md        # frontmatter + markdown body\n│   └── skills.md      # optional: skill list\n├── researcher/\n│   ├── role.md\n│   └── skills.md\n├── tester/\n│   └── role.md\n└── ...\n```\n\n**role.md frontmatter format:**\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: developer\ndescription: Writes code, fixes bugs, writes tests\narchetype: implementer          # implementer | analyst | reviewer | tester\nstart_status: development       # FIRST child command sets this status after launch\nend_status: to-review           # role handoff status; for producers this means ready for review, not done\ndod:                            # definition of done — added to board checklist on spawn\n  - Code written per task description and AC\n  - text: Relevant tests written for new or changed behavior and passing\n    classes: [code]\n  - text: Relevant build/validation commands run after changes and build not broken\n    classes: [code]\nskills:                         # optional: inline skill list (merged with skills.md)\n  - go-tui-testing-tools\n---\n\n# developer\n\n## Description\nWrites code — features, bugfixes, refactoring. Writes tests for the code produced.\n\n## Standing Orders\n1. Add or update relevant tests for changed behavior.\n2. Run the relevant tests before handoff.\n3. Run the relevant build or validation command after changes.\n\n## Deliverable\nCode + tests.\n...\n```\n\nEvery board element has a `task_class`: `code` (the default), `metadata`, `docs`, or `research`. Set it with `create(..., task_class=metadata)` or `set_details(ID, task_class=metadata)`, and inspect it through the `task_class` q projection.\n\nRole `dod` entries may stay as plain strings, which apply to every task class, or use an object with `text` and `classes`. Spawn injects object entries only when the element's `task_class` appears in `classes`; an omitted or empty `classes` list preserves the legacy all-classes behavior.\n\n**skills.md format** (optional, merged with `skills:` in frontmatter):\n```markdown\n## Required\n- project-management\n- go-tui-testing-tools\n\n## Domain\n- core-data (when working on persistence)\n- swiftui (when building UI)\n```\n\n**Resolution order** (`LoadRole`):\n1. Project-local directory: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>/role.md`\n2. Project-local single file: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>.md`\n3. Global directory: `~/.roles/<name>/role.md`\n4. Global single file: `~/.roles/<name>.md`\n\nDirectory format takes priority over single file. Project-local takes priority over global.\n\n**Global role installation:** `scripts/setup.sh` copies the bundled roles from `.roles/` into `~/.roles/`, making them available in any project without a local `.roles/` directory.\n\n**Available roles:**\n\n| Role | Archetype | Start → End Status |\n|------|-----------|-------------------|\n| `researcher` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\n| `developer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\n| `tester` | tester | `development` → `to-review` |\n| `solution-architect` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\n| `doc-writer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\n| `reviewer` | reviewer | `reviewing` → verdict-driven (`done` / `to-dev` / `analysis` / `blocked`) |\n| `orchestrator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\n| `coordinator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\n\nThe reviewer role intentionally has no unconditional `end_status`. Its role body selects exactly one verdict branch, so the default spawn template cannot overwrite a requested-changes or stop-the-line verdict with `done`. Only an accepted review sets `done`.\n\n### How Roles Work with Spawn\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** Spawning without a role produces a crippled agent with no identity, skills, DoD, or lifecycle commands. Model and reasoning are deliberate caller choices and never inherit task-board defaults. `--agent` remains optional: pass a human/task-context provider only when the effective `preferred_agentic_system` allows it; omission uses the exclusive pin, an in-set mixed affinity, legacy preference, or plain affinity as applicable.\n\nThe spawn pipeline:\n1. Loads role config from `.roles/<role>/role.md`\n2. Loads task metadata (README + precondition resources)\n3. Computes pre-launch board updates:\n   - Sets agent name with archetype prefix, e.g. `[implementer] developer (codex)`\n   - Adds DoD items as checklist entries on the board element\n4. Assembles prompt from template: role body, skills, DoD, status commands, task metadata\n5. Persists prompt and spawn-run state under `.temp/prompts/` and `.temp/spawn-runs/RUN-.../`\n6. Returns the `RUN-...` handle immediately\n7. Background runner claims a queue slot under `spawn.max_parallel`, launches the real child agent, streams logwork, and executes completion hooks\n8. Agent gets FIRST command (set `start_status` after launch) and LAST role-handoff command (set end status) in prompt\n\n```bash\n# Spawn researcher for a research task (status → analysis)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn developer for implementation (status → development, DoD → checklist)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Agent type follows the active schedule window, canonical provider system, legacy preference, then runtime affinity; model/reasoning remain explicit\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-xyz --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn reviewer (status → reviewing)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn orchestrator/coordinator for decomposition / orchestration / verification\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role orchestrator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role coordinator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n```\n\n`orchestrator` / `coordinator` is the special supervisor role: it decomposes settled specs into board elements inline, creates or refines tasks, spawns specialists, guards architecture, and owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until accepted `done`. It must not create a board-layout meta-task, stop at `to-review`, ask the human to perform routine review, or do specialist implementation directly when delegation is appropriate. Spawn `solution-architect` only for genuine architecture decisions, not to perform the board layout.\n\nFor coordinator orchestration:\n- fan out children with explicit role/model selections; inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system`, omit `--agent` for exclusive/in-set-affinity resolution, and pass it only for an allowed human/task-context provider\n- keep the returned `RUN-...` ids\n- use `task-board spawn status/wait` as explicit synchronization points\n- sequence dependent work: wait for producer runs, verify outcome resources, then spawn reviewer/consumer runs\n- on requested changes, preserve the verdict, spawn focused rework, and send the next handoff through another reviewer until accepted `done`\n- if a child exits before handoff or parks in an intermediate status, inspect its run/outcomes and retry or reroute through a focused child; do not hand recoverable work to the human\n- use `blocked` only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision, after recording evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, the recommendation, and the exact decision or external input needed\n- do not shell-background `task-board spawn`; that duplicates the async layer and makes completion handling worse\n\n### Skill Injection\n\nSkills are injected as references (paths), not inlined into prompts. Claude, Codex, and Qwen use lazy body loading — only skill metadata (name + description) loads at startup (~50-200 tokens per skill). Treat spawned-role skill lists as lazy catalogs: full SKILL.md bodies load only when relevant to the concrete task scope, unless the user, task, or project explicitly requires one.\n\nThis means one `developer` role can reference many skills (swiftui, core-data, go-testing-tools, etc.) without context bloat — the agent picks what's needed for the specific task. See [`references/context-efficiency.md`](references/context-efficiency.md) for the selection rule.\n\n## Sub-Agent Prompt Templates\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
